// Native canonical-IPA data manifest for Abkhaz (ab) — аҧсуа бызшәа, NORTHWEST CAUCASIAN (~190k, Abkhazia).
// One of the LARGEST CONSONANT INVENTORIES in the world (~58, with LABIALIZED, PALATALIZED, PHARYNGEALIZED
// and EJECTIVE series) and only TWO phonemic vowels (⟨а⟩→[a], ⟨ы⟩→[ə]). The Cyrillic alphabet writes the
// consonants with base letters + MODIFIER letters:
//   ⚠ ⟨ь⟩ PALATALIZES to a dorsal+[ʲ] (гь→[ɡʲ], хь→[χʲ]), ⟨ә⟩ LABIALIZES (гә→[ɡʷ], шә→[ʃʷ]), ⟨'⟩
//     PHARYNGEALIZES (х'→[χˤ]).
//   ⚠ THREE-WAY stops/affricates — voiced / aspirated / ejective: ⟨г қ к⟩→[ɡ kʰ kʼ], ⟨д ҭ т⟩→[d tʰ tʼ],
//     ⟨б ҧ п⟩→[b pʰ pʼ], ⟨ӡ ц ҵ⟩→[d͡z t͡sʰ t͡sʼ], ⟨џ ч ҷ⟩→[d͡ʐ t͡ʃʰ t͡ʃʼ]; the uvular ⟨ҟ⟩→[qʼ], the
//     pharyngeal ⟨ҳ⟩→[ħ].
// The scan (abkhaz.ts) is longest-match over these tables — trigraph, then base+modifier, then base — with
// the ⟨у⟩/⟨и⟩ glide-vs-syllabic split in code. Numbers are VIGESIMAL (base-20), composed by numbers.ts.
// ⚠ THE REFEREE IS PARTLY CIRCULAR. wikipron abk_cyrl and kaikki Abkhaz both cover this language well, but
// much of what they carry is the letter and digraph DEFINITIONS this engine is built from — so agreement
// with them is close to reference-parity rather than independent confirmation. Their narrow transcriptions
// are also internally inconsistent, which is why the tables here follow the corpus where the two disagree.
{
    "language": "ab",
    "name": "Abkhaz (аҧсуа бызшәа)",
    "script": ["Cyrillic"],

    // Base letter + MODIFIER (⟨ь⟩ palatal / ⟨ә⟩ labial / ⟨'⟩ pharyngeal) → the specific IPA cluster (from
    // the Wiktionary letter-definitions). Longest-match: trigraphs (х'ә) before 2-char before base. Any
    // base+ь/ә not here falls back to base+[ʲ]/[ʷ] (handled in abkhaz.ts).
    "clusters": {
        "х'ә": "χˤʷ",
        // ⚠ Palatalized DORSALS are dorsal + [ʲ], NOT the devoiced palatal-place symbols the wikipron
        // letter-definitions give: the corpus uses ɡʲ/kʼʲ/kʰʲ/χʲ, matching the already-correct ҟь→qʼʲ and
        // ӷь→ʁʲ. ⟨г⟩ is voiced, so гь cannot be [c].
        "гь": "ɡʲ", "гә": "ɡʷ", "дә": "dʷ", "жь": "ʒ", "жә": "ʒʷ", "кь": "kʼʲ", "кә": "kʼʷ", "тә": "tʷʼ",
        "хь": "χʲ", "хә": "χʷ", "ць": "t͡ɕʰ", "цә": "t͡ɕʷʰ", "шь": "ʃ", "шә": "ʃʷ", "ҙә": "ʑʷ",
        "қь": "kʰʲ", "қә": "kʷʰ", "ҟь": "qʼʲ", "ҟә": "qʼʷ", "ҭә": "tʷʰ", "ҳә": "ħʷ", "ҵь": "t͡ɕʼ", "ҵә": "t͡ɕʼʷ",
        "ӡь": "d͡ʑ", "ӡә": "d͡ʑʷ", "ӷь": "ʁʲ", "ӷә": "ʁʷ", "ҕь": "ʁʲ", "ҕә": "ʁʷ", "ҫә": "ɕʷ", "џь": "d͡ʒ",
        "чь": "t͡ɕʰ", "ҷь": "t͡ɕʼ", "х'": "χˤ",
    },

    // Base letters (single Cyrillic). The three-way stop/affricate series + the NW-Caucasian extras.
    "base": {
        "а": "a", "б": "b", "в": "v", "г": "ɡ", "д": "d", "е": "e", "ж": "ʐ", "з": "z", "и": "i", "й": "j",
        "к": "kʼ", "л": "l", "м": "m", "н": "n", "о": "o", "п": "pʼ", "р": "r", "с": "s", "т": "tʼ", "у": "w",
        "ф": "f", "х": "χ", "ц": "t͡sʰ", "ч": "t͡ʃʰ", "ш": "ʂ", "ы": "ə", "э": "e", "ю": "ju", "я": "ja",
        "ҕ": "ʁ", "ӷ": "ʁ", "ҙ": "ʑ", "ӡ": "d͡z", "қ": "kʰ", "ӄ": "kʰ", "ҟ": "qʼ", "ҧ": "pʰ", "ԥ": "pʰ",
        "ҩ": "ɥ", "ҫ": "ɕ", "ҭ": "tʰ", "ҳ": "ħ", "ҵ": "t͡sʼ", "ҷ": "t͡ʃʼ", "ҽ": "t͡ʂʰ", "ҿ": "t͡ʂʼ", "џ": "d͡ʐ",
        // Extended / historical Abkhaz Cyrillic letters (older orthographies):
        "ԡ": "lʰ", "ԣ": "nʰ", "ԫ": "d͡ʒ", "ԭ": "d͡ʑ",
        "ꚁ": "dʷ", "ꚃ": "d͡ʑʷ", "ꚅ": "ʒʷ", "ꚇ": "t͡ʂʰ", "ꚉ": "d͡z", "ꚋ": "tʰ", "ꚍ": "tʷʰ", "ꚏ": "t͡ɕʷʰ",
        "ꚑ": "t͡sʼ", "ꚓ": "t͡ʃʼ", "ꚕ": "ħʷ", "ꚗ": "ʃʷ",
        // ⟨ъ⟩ hard sign dropped; ⟨ь⟩/⟨ә⟩ handled as modifiers in abkhaz.ts.
    },

    // A modifier standing after a base with no CLUSTER entry → the generic modifier IPA is appended.
    "modifiers": { "ь": "ʲ", "ә": "ʷ", "'": "ˤ" },

    // The VOWEL LETTERS — the two phonemic vowels ⟨а ы⟩ plus the letters that write a vowel in loans and in
    // the ⟨е о⟩/⟨и у⟩ series. This is the ENVIRONMENT for the ⟨у⟩/⟨и⟩ glide-vs-syllabic rule in abkhaz.ts:
    // both are underlyingly the glides /w j/, realized [w]/[j] beside a vowel and syllabic [u]/[i] between
    // consonants or word-finally (аи→aj, аԥсуа→apʰswa; but иҭабуп→itʰabup, амени→ameni). ⟨у⟩ and ⟨и⟩ are
    // themselves in the set, so a sequence of the two counts each other as the vowel context.
    "vowelLetters": ["а", "ы", "е", "о", "у", "и"],

    // VIGESIMAL cardinal numbers — the traditional NW-Caucasian base-20 system (composition logic in
    // numbers.ts). The four score words are 20 ҩажәа, 40 ҩынҩажәа (2×20), 60 хынҩажәа (3×20),
    // 80 ԥшьынҩажәа (4×20). An exact multiple of 20 is just that word; anything else is the score's
    // CONNECTIVE form (final -а → -и, "and") plus a SPACE plus the plain 1–19 word — so, exactly as in
    // Georgian, the whole 1–19 series INCLUDING THE TEENS attaches into one slot:
    //     30 ҩажәи жәаба (20+10) · 45 ҩынҩажәи хәба (2×20+5) · 67 хынҩажәи быжьба (3×20+7)
    //     89 ԥшьынҩажәи жәба (4×20+9) · 99 ԥшьынҩажәи зеижә (4×20+19)
    // ⚠ THERE IS NO "TEN" DIGIT: 50 is ҩынҩажәи жәаба (2×20+10), 70 хынҩажәи жәаба, 90 ԥшьынҩажәи жәаба.
    // ⚠ THE SAME -и CONNECTIVE MARKS A NON-FINAL HUNDRED: 100 шәкы but 101 шәи акы; 200 ҩышә but
    //   201 ҩышәи акы. Stored as bare/comb pairs below. The THOUSAND word does NOT take it (1001 зқьы акы,
    //   2001 ҩнызқь акы).
    // ⚠ THOUSANDS ARE FUSED for a multiplier of 1–10 (зқьы, ҩнызқь, хнызқь … жәанызқь) and for exactly 100
    //   (шәнызқь); any other multiplier is spelled out plus the separate word нызқь (20 000 ҩажәа нызқь).
    //
    // ⚠ CITATION FORM / CLASS AGREEMENT — the one real judgment call. Abkhaz numerals agree with the HUMAN vs
    // NON-HUMAN class of what they count: the human series is built with -ҩык/-џьара (аӡәы "one person",
    // ҩыџьа "two people"), the non-human/abstract series is акы, ҩба, хԥа … A bare numeral in a TTS input
    // string has NO counted noun and therefore no class to agree with, so the words here are the NON-HUMAN
    // series throughout — which is also the series ab.wikipedia's own number articles use to NAME the
    // numbers, i.e. the citation form a speaker reads a bare digit string with. Human-class concord would
    // need the noun and is out of scope.
    //
    // SOURCE: ab.wikipedia «Иԥсабаратәу ахыԥхьаӡара» (Natural number), whose 0–99 table, hundreds list and
    // thousands list are cited there to Хәарцкиа Ҳ. И. & Џьонуа Б. Гь., «АУРЫС-АԤСУА, АԤСУА-АУРЫС
    // акомпиутертә терминқәа ржәар», Аҟәа 2012. Cross-checked word-for-word against ab.wikipedia's own
    // per-number and year articles across the 21–99, 101–199, 201–999 and 1001–2020 ranges. Zero is from
    // «Аноль».
    // Other judgment calls: 7 is authored быжьба (the year articles and Omniglot) where the dictionary table
    // writes the syncopated бжьба, which also surfaces in the derived 7000 бжьнызқь — both are current, so
    // the fuller stem is used for the standalone numeral and the source's own form for each derived word.
    // 500 is authored хәышә per the hundreds list, though the 555/1500 year articles write the syncopated
    // хәшә(и). 10⁶ / 10⁹ use the Russian loans миллион / миллиард, attested in running ab.wikipedia text; a
    // count of 1 is read as the bare noun, parallel to зқьы for 1000.
    // ⚠ TEXT-NORMALIZATION DATA (normalize.ts). Every string here is spelled out somewhere in
    // tools/corpus/mined/ab.jsonc — nothing is invented, and the counts are in the rule comments.
    // ⟨ш.⟩ is the year abbreviation and ⟨ашә.⟩ the century one; ⟨шш.⟩ is the plural year form. LONGEST
    // FIRST, because the scan applies them in order and ⟨ш⟩ would otherwise eat the stem of ⟨шш⟩/⟨ашә⟩.
    // ⚠ ⟨ш.ш.⟩ IS A SPELLING OF ⟨шш.⟩ (×3 against ×12) and needs its own row: the abbreviation dot is not
    // a letter, so a rule keyed on ⟨ш⟩ alone matches BOTH halves and yields *шықәсашықәса.
    "abbreviations": [
        ["ашә", "ашәышықәса"],
        ["ш.ш", "шықәсақәа"],
        ["шш", "шықәсақәа"],
        ["ш", "шықәса"]
    ],

    "numbers": {
        "units": ["аноль", "акы", "ҩба", "хԥа", "ԥшьба", "хәба", "фба", "быжьба", "ааба", "жәба"],
        "teens": [
            "жәаба", "жәеиза", "жәаҩа", "жәаха", "жәиԥшь",
            "жәохә", "жәаф", "жәибжь", "жәаа", "зеижә",
        ],
        // Indexed by the SCORE count 1–4 (= 20, 40, 60, 80); index 0 is unused padding. "comb" is the
        // connective form (final -а → -и) used when a 1–19 remainder follows.
        "scores": {
            "bare": ["", "ҩажәа", "ҩынҩажәа", "хынҩажәа", "ԥшьынҩажәа"],
            "comb": ["", "ҩажәи", "ҩынҩажәи", "хынҩажәи", "ԥшьынҩажәи"],
        },
        // Indexed by the hundreds digit 1–9; index 0 is unused padding. "comb" again takes -и (шәкы → шәи акы).
        "hundreds": {
            "bare": ["", "шәкы", "ҩышә", "хышә", "ԥшьышә", "хәышә", "фышә", "быжьшәы", "аашәы", "жәшәы"],
            "comb": ["", "шәи", "ҩышәи", "хышәи", "ԥшьышәи", "хәышәи", "фышәи", "быжьшәи", "аашәи", "жәшәи"],
        },
        // "fused" is indexed by the thousands multiplier 1–10 (index 0 unused padding); "hundred" is the
        // likewise-fused 100 000; "word" is the separate нызқь used with any other multiplier.
        "thousands": {
            "fused": [
                "", "зқьы", "ҩнызқь", "хнызқь", "ԥшьнызқь", "хәнызқь",
                "фнызқь", "бжьнызқь", "аанызқь", "жәнызқь", "жәанызқь",
            ],
            "hundred": "шәнызқь",
            "word": "нызқь",
        },
        "million": "миллион",
        "milliard": "миллиард",

        // ⚠ THE ORDINAL IS а + CARDINAL + тәи — proved by the corpus's own spelled forms аҩбатәи (2),
        // ахԥатәи (3), аԥшьбатәи (4), афбатәи (6), ажәабатәи (10), against the written numeral form
        // 6-тәи / 19-тәи.
        // ⚠ 1 IS SUPPLETIVE: актәи, never *акытәи, which the cardinal акы would otherwise give. Five
        // corpus instances.
        "ordinalSuffix": "тәи",
        "ordinalOne": "актәи",

        // The range connectives, both written out in the corpus in this exact frame — "16-тәи ашәышықәса
        // ИНАРКНЫ 19-тәи ашәышықәса алагамҭа АҞЫНӠА". инаркны ×25, рҟынӡа ×25.
        "rangeFrom": "инаркны",
        "rangeTo": "рҟынӡа",
    },

    // ⚠ SYMBOL VOCABULARY (normalize.ts). Unlike everything above, these words are NOT in the mined corpus
    // artifact — corpus-words.ts finds none of them in its 7,780 tokens, because the artifact samples 208
    // excerpts. They ARE attested in the FULL ab.wikipedia text (search API, 2026-08-08), which is the same
    // source register the corpus was mined from. Counts and verbatim frames are in
    // docs/abkhaz_vocabulary_investigation.md; the ones that fix the word ORDER are:
    //   процент ×10 — always straight after the numeral: "18 процент", "жәаба процент".
    //   градус ×9 — postposed bare: "180 градус", "2,5 градус". ⟨Цельси иградус⟩ is the СИ article's own
    //     name for degree Celsius (possessive и-, "Celsius's degree") — the scale word is NEVER attested
    //     bare after a number, so the unit name is used verbatim.
    //   асааҭ ×19 — the clock word goes BEFORE the number: "асааҭ 6 рзы", "асааҭ 18:21:56 рзы".
    //   "8 миллиард доллар" (Формула Аку) — number, scale, currency word, in that order. доллар ×41,
    //     евро ×37, фунт ×10; ⟨фунт стерлинг⟩ is spelled out for the £ sums ("29,721,250 фунт стерлинг" —
    //     the same sum this repo's corpus carries as £29,721,250).
    //   "143,600 километра квадрат (55,400 миль квадрат)" (Башкирия) — км² has a spelled form. ⚠ ONLY the
    //     square: no cubic word is attested anywhere (куб ×0), so м³/см³/км³ stay deferred.
    //   млрд/млн are the corpus's own scale abbreviations ("$1,86 млрд", "€ 30 млн"); their expansions are
    //     the миллиард/миллион already in "numbers" above.
    "symbols": {
        "percent": "процент",
        "degree": "градус",
        "celsius": "Цельси иградус",
        "hour": "асааҭ",
        "squareKm": "километра квадрат",
        // [symbol, word] — the symbol PRECEDES the number in text ($1,86) but the word FOLLOWS it in speech.
        // ⚠ THE LETTER-PREFIXED KEYS ARE LOAD-BEARING: the shared symbol tier letter-bounds a bare sign so
        // it cannot split a code prefix, which means ⟨US$30⟩ and the corpus's own ⟨B£20⟩ (the Brixton
        // pound — "брикстонтәи афунтқәа") match NOTHING unless the compound key is declared. Longest-first
        // matching is the tier's, not ours.
        "currencies": [["US$", "доллар"], ["B£", "фунт"], ["$", "доллар"], ["€", "евро"], ["£", "фунт стерлинг"]],
        // Scale abbreviation → the KEY IN "numbers" ABOVE that holds its word — a reference, not a copy,
        // so миллиард/миллион cannot drift apart between the number path and the currency path.
        "scales": { "млрд": "milliard", "млн": "million" },
    },
}
